Upstream-Name: sizer
Comment:
 Sizer itself is MIT licensed, see the Files paragraph below.
 .
 Every component listed here was read from its own copyright file in the
 distribution it ships with; the license named is the one that file states,
 and the wording records how Sizer relates to that component.
 .
 Linked into /usr/bin/sizer (each one appears in `ldd /usr/bin/sizer`):
 .
   * Xft, source package libxft2, /usr/share/doc/libxft2/copyright:
     MIT/X11-style permission notice, Copyright (c) 2001,2003 Keith Packard.
     Dynamically linked as libXft.so.2.
   * fontconfig, source package libfontconfig1,
     /usr/share/doc/libfontconfig1/copyright: MIT/X11-style permission
     notice, Copyright (c) 2001,2003 Keith Packard. Dynamically linked as
     libfontconfig.so.1.
   * libX11, source package libx11-6, /usr/share/doc/libx11-6/copyright:
     the X.Org standard copyright, a MIT-style permissive license.
     Dynamically linked as libX11.so.6.
   * FreeType, source package libfreetype6,
     /usr/share/doc/libfreetype6/copyright: FTL and Expat. Dynamically
     linked as libfreetype.so.6 through Xft.
 .
 Present in the process that hosts the extension, but not linked into Sizer:
 .
   * GNOME Shell, source package gnome-shell,
     /usr/share/doc/gnome-shell/copyright: GPL-2+ (parts LGPL-2+,
     LGPL-2.1). The extension under
     /usr/share/gnome-shell/extensions/sizer@sizerlinux.dev is GJS source
     that the running GNOME Shell loads and interprets; it imports Shell's
     own JS modules (resource:///org/gnome/shell/...) and exports one D-Bus
     interface. It is not compiled against, linked to, or derived from GNOME
     Shell, so GPL-2+ does not extend to it.
   * GTK 4, source package libgtk-4-1, /usr/share/doc/libgtk-4-1/copyright:
     LGPL-2.1+. Not linked by Sizer; the GNOME Shell process loads it, and
     the extension calls no GTK API.
   * libadwaita, source package libadwaita-1-0,
     /usr/share/doc/libadwaita-1-0/copyright: LGPL-2.1+ (GPL-3+ where
     combined). Not linked and not loaded: nothing in Sizer imports it.
 .
 The Rust dependencies statically linked into /usr/bin/sizer are recorded,
 with their license identifiers, in the project's Cargo.lock.

